Here is a reproducer for a slightly different version of this issue:
```rust
fn f(a : &mut [S; 8]) {
    let mut i = 0;
    while i < 8 {
        a[i].0 = 0;
        i += 1;
    }
}
```

which produces the following lean:
```lean
def f_loop.body
  (a : Array S 8#usize) (i : Std.Usize) :
  Result (ControlFlow ((Array S 8#usize) × Std.Usize) (Array S 8#usize))
  := do
  if i < 8#usize
  then let a1 ← Array.update a i ()
       let i1 ← i + 1#usize
       ok (cont (a1, i1))
  else ok (done a)
```

where `Array.update a i ()` is incorrect

Other reproducer:

```rust
trait T {
    type Output;
    fn f(&mut self) -> Self::Output; 
}

impl T for u16 {
    type Output = ();
    fn f(&mut self) -> () {
    }
}
```
The issue seems to come from the [`ignore_output` simplification](https://github.com/AeneasVerif/aeneas/blob/a2fcf1923d16684cec638f5503da11acd0ef002d/src/symbolic/SymbolicToPureExpressions.ml#L384) that omits the `Unit` from the return type which doesn't play well with traits where the shape of the return value must be the same for all impls. We could deactivate the optimization for trait methods, or at least for trait methods where the output type comes from a generic or associated type.
